  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand when the greedy algorithm (which chooses the largest possible coins first) will give us the optimal solution for any given amount \\(N\\). This happens when \\(N\\) is a multiple of 25, because the greedy algorithm will always choose 25-cent coins first. If \\(N\\) is not a multiple of 25, then there exists another combination of coins that uses fewer coins to achieve the same value.\n\nWe will iterate through all values of \\(N\\) from 1 to 1000 and check if the greedy algorithm succeeds for each value. The algorithm succeeds if \\(N\\) is a multiple of 25. We will count the number of such values.\n\nLet's write the code to find the answer.\n```python\n# Initialize the counter for the number of values for which the greedy algorithm succeeds\r\ncount = 0\r\n\r\n# Iterate through all values of N from 1 to 1000\r\nfor N in range(1, 1001):\r\n    # Check if N is a multiple of 25\r\n    if N % 25 == 0:\r\n        # If it is, the greedy algorithm succeeds for this value of N\r\n        count += 1\r\n\r\n# The final answer is the number of values of N for which the greedy algorithm succeeds\r\nprint(count)\n```\n```output\n40\n```\nThe number of values of \\(N\\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
